WEBVTT

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:06.090
Cables are traditional favorites
for both machine and hand knitters.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.850
Here are a few tips for success
on your knitting machine.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:17.050
The basic move to cross cables
is to use two transfer tools,

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:23.090
remove the first set of stitches on one
of the tools and turn it 90 degrees.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.490
Then remove and transfer
the adjoining stitches.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:34.080
Replace the held stitches on the empty
needles and your cable is crossed.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.440
Because our knitting is stretched across
the needle bed, it can be

00:00:38.506 --> 00:00:41.890
difficult to form cables sometimes.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.410
Using a looser tension than you might
normally choose for your yarn

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.760
and knitting with a yarn
that's a bit stretchy can help.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.890
Following are some other
suggestions that will help as well.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.880
Now, let's get started with this example,
and we're going to cross a three by three

00:01:01.946 --> 00:01:05.730
cable, three stitches over three stitches.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.730
Now, you can see the strain on the
stitches as they're being transferred.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.730
And if you look closely, the needles
are also pulled a bit out of alignment.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.920
That can cause some problems knitting.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.600
Working two by two cables would be easier,
but there are things you can do

00:01:24.666 --> 00:01:28.840
to improve this three by three.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.850
Looking at on the right side of the work,
you have the texture of the cables,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.090
but something's missing.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.530
Normally, you see a column or two of pearl
stitches on either side of the cable,

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.880
giving the fabric much more texture
and making those cables pop.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.800
There's an easy way for machine knitters
to accomplish this look by simply leaving

00:01:53.866 --> 00:01:57.650
a needle out of work
on each side of the cable.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:02.690
In our example, we're again
a three by three cable.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.050
We have a needle out of work,
there are six needles in work,

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.840
and then a needle out of work.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.330
This time when we cross our cable,
there's less strain on those edge

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.840
stitches, and the cables
are a bit easier to cross.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.080
Looking at the finish side,
this is a traditional looking cable

00:02:22.146 --> 00:02:26.080
that seems to pop right off
the surface of the knitting.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:32.130
But you may not like the ladders
along the edge of your cables.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.120
Here's a better method.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.440
I've identified and marked
the needles for my cable.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:49.640
I'm going to drop one stitch on either
side of the six cable needles.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.760
Now, when I cross my cable, there's a lot
of slack, and the crossing isn't tight.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.054
But you'll see that those
stitches have run down.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.120
Now,

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.370
before I knit the next row,

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.040
I need to latch up those drop stitches,
forming a column of pearl stitches

00:03:16.106 --> 00:03:19.480
on either side of the cable.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.240
So after you latch up,
just rehang those single stitches

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:29.600
on the empty needle,
and then you're ready

00:03:29.666 --> 00:03:32.080
to knit across the row.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:44.760
I'm knitting eight rows, and then
I'm going to cross another cable.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.850
Now, here's a really neat trick.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:53.480
Before I drop the stitches on each side
of the cable, I use a stitch marker or

00:03:53.546 --> 00:03:59.440
a Knitter's coil-less pin and secure
the last stitch that I manually

00:03:59.506 --> 00:04:02.410
formed on the previous cable.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.960
Now, when I drop those stitches,
they're only going to run down

00:04:06.026 --> 00:04:10.600
to the point of the marker,
and then I can grab that stitch

00:04:10.666 --> 00:04:14.440
and more easily relatch the column.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.490
Looking at the cables from the right side,
they are beautiful.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:26.160
No large ladders and a neat pearl column
makes that cable really stand out.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.600
If you're following a pattern with cables,
remember that machine knitters are viewing

00:04:31.666 --> 00:04:35.250
their knitting from the private
or the wrong side of the work.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:37.970
You have the pearl side facing you.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.370
So study your stitch chart carefully.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:44.930
If you're following a hand knitting cable
pattern,

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.600
They're drawn as if you're viewing
the work from the public or

00:04:48.666 --> 00:04:51.000
the right side of the work.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:57.960
In this example, with this chart,
the cable will cross from left to right.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:03.000
Again, read your pattern carefully
and study the direction of the cross

00:05:03.066 --> 00:05:05.050
before starting a garment.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:11.160
Better yet, work a swatch to confirm
the direction of the crosses.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:19.050
If you love cables and would like
to learn more, we recommend four books.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.080
Susan Guayumi's Hand Manipulated Stitches
for Machine Knitters

00:05:22.146 --> 00:05:25.560
and More Hand Manipulated Stitches

00:05:25.626 --> 00:05:30.760
books are invaluable and inspirational.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:35.490
Everything you ever wanted
to know about cables.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.450
Take time to try cables for yourself.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.440
Have fun and happy knitting.

